            </p>
            <p>
              My research investigates the internal computational mechanisms of modern neural
              networks. Using causal interventions, representation geometry, and
              neuroscience-inspired ablation protocols, I study how language and vision-language
              models represent tasks, transform residual activations, and route long-range context.
            </p>
            <p>
              That line of inquiry grew out of systems friction while building <em>AgentFox</em> (a
              local agentic browser) and <em>Trinetra</em>: even when an LLM ingests only selected
              video frames, quadratic transformer attention still bottlenecks scaling. Today, standard
              inference burns the same forward pass evaluating <code>3 + 7</code> as synthesizing
              production code.
            </p>
            <p>
              My core question is narrower and empirically falsifiable: <strong>find the minimal
              causal circuit doing task-relevant work, and you naturally obtain both zero-training
